#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176692} :activity/id #uuid "def4663c-59d2-5447-81ce-d279fab17096" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048837 :topic/id #uuid "c4d057b8-12ac-5f2a-94f7-d0b239050514" :topic/math-academy-id 3560} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176674 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175948 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175805 :question/id #uuid "7d2aa3e0-f6de-5e11-95d8-8877a87b13db" :question/math-academy-id "e-749"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfc0d9e1-bce1-5d62-b3c8-f7d7bb226497" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050624}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176358} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176363} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244100} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244295} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269178} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319185} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320892} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320893} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399384} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530450} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530475} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530481} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530497}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius and Interval of Convergence of a Power Series"} :step/id #uuid "6d91432f-0bfa-58bd-808c-9a2779a7ca28" :step/math-academy-id 2557 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176683}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176683 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175956 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175802 :question/id #uuid "7b5adbc9-3fa0-5388-8b62-9053147fbfe5" :question/math-academy-id "e-7227"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "60f61c26-ff2d-5343-becd-d8f44f623cd3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176507} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237976} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237977} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332833} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332834} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332835} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332836} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332837} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530462} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530482}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Convergence of a Power Series"} :step/id #uuid "e98c072b-028e-5af9-99ec-59557e741afb" :step/math-academy-id 26731} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176692 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176978 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have the following infinite power series.\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{(x-2)^n}{n^2} = (x-2) + \\dfrac{(x-2)^2}{2^2} + \\dfrac{(x-2)^3}{3^2} + \\cdots\n$$\n\nHow do we determine the radius and interval of convergence for this series?\n\nFirst, note that the terms of the series are all powers of $(x-2).$ For this reason, we say that the series is **centered** at $x=2.$\n\nTo compute the radius of convergence, we use the ratio test for convergence. We define \n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{(x-2)^n}{n^2},\n$$\n\nand we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(x-2)^{n+1}}{(n+1)^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{{n}^{2}}{(x-2)^{n}}| \\\\  & =|x-2|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{n}^{2}}{(n+1)^{2}}) \\\\  & =|x-2|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{n}^{2}}{{n}^{2}+2n+1}) \\\\  & =|x-2|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{(1+\\frac{2}{n}+\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}})}) \\\\  & =|x-2|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =|x-2|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if \n\n$$\nL < {\\color{blue}1}\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\left| x-{\\color{red}2} \\right| < {\\color{blue}1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n- the radius of convergence is ${\\color{blue}1},$ and\n\n- the interval of convergence is centered at the point $x={\\color{red}2}.$\n\nTo find the interval of convergence, we solve the inequality $\\left| x-2 \\right| < 1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x-2| & <1 \\\\ -1<x-2 & <1 \\\\ 1<x & <3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the series converges for any $x$ in the open interval $(1,3).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series at the endpoints of this interval. So, we need to investigate the endpoints $x=1$ and $x=3$ separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- We start with the left endpoint. When $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x-2)^{n}}{{n}^{2}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(1-2)^{n}}{{n}^{2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}} \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{1}{16}+⋯,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test.\n\n- We then look at the right endpoint. When $x=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x-2)^{n}}{{n}^{2}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(3-2)^{n}}{{n}^{2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{1}^{n}}{{n}^{2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{1}{16}+⋯,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the $p$-series test.\n\nFinally, we conclude that the interval of convergence is $I=[1,3].$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3711e5cb-ff53-5f13-a62a-da24f59e888b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5191 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "638d27e8-a48f-5883-adc8-de19b25a5a0e" :step/math-academy-id 32549 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176674}}] :activity/title "Radius of Convergence of Power Series" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176111}]}
